WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.478 --> 00:00:02.342
- When these music requests
come in for Rick and Morty,

00:00:02.342 --> 00:00:04.156
I never know what to expect.

00:00:04.156 --> 00:00:07.395
- I, I, I need to know what a
frog planet's instruments

00:00:07.395 --> 00:00:09.203
would sound like.

00:00:09.203 --> 00:00:13.939
It's like an intergalactic race
riot, but it's a love story.

00:00:13.939 --> 00:00:17.439
I need a royal march for an insect species

00:00:18.729 --> 00:00:21.197
that thinks it's koalas.

00:00:21.197 --> 00:00:22.637
- I write all the music
for Rick and Morty.

00:00:22.637 --> 00:00:25.708
In fact, my name's in
the credits, hold on.

00:00:25.708 --> 00:00:26.738
(electronic music)

00:00:26.738 --> 00:00:29.238
(bell chimes)

00:00:31.085 --> 00:00:34.168
(otherworldly music)

00:00:39.767 --> 00:00:43.100
(soft electronic music)

00:00:47.163 --> 00:00:49.036
Take for example in
season one, episode two,

00:00:49.036 --> 00:00:53.119
I had to score this alien
bondage dream sequence.

00:00:56.125 --> 00:00:57.622
It took awhile to figure
it out, but once I realized

00:00:57.622 --> 00:01:00.245
I could use this Gregorian
chant as a background element,

00:01:00.245 --> 00:01:02.283
really came together.

00:01:02.283 --> 00:01:04.666
(camera shutter clicks)

00:01:04.666 --> 00:01:07.276
So you know the episode Get
Schwifty, that alien band

00:01:07.276 --> 00:01:08.926
that gets annihilated.

00:01:08.926 --> 00:01:10.710
I had to make it sound like
they were playing instruments

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:13.848
from another planet using
only instruments from Earth.

00:01:13.848 --> 00:01:15.632
So I used a bunch of
percussion instruments

00:01:15.632 --> 00:01:18.178
from all over the world.

00:01:18.178 --> 00:01:19.644
My favorite song I
wrote for Rick and Morty

00:01:19.644 --> 00:01:22.874
was Goodbye Moon Men, which is
kind of a David Bowie thing,

00:01:22.874 --> 00:01:24.897
and actually, if you listen
close, you can hear me

00:01:24.897 --> 00:01:26.653
singing background vocals.

00:01:26.653 --> 00:01:27.928
Here, I'll prove it.

00:01:27.928 --> 00:01:31.334
♫ Goodbye moon men

00:01:31.334 --> 00:01:35.625
♫ You say goodbye moon men

00:01:35.625 --> 00:01:37.792
♫ Goodbye

00:01:40.203 --> 00:01:42.453
♫ Moon men

00:01:43.927 --> 00:01:46.091
I left a couple of musical
Easter eggs in the show,

00:01:46.091 --> 00:01:47.880
and I'll tell you about one of them.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:49.971
In season one, I wrote a
song called Human Music,

00:01:49.971 --> 00:01:52.297
and you can hear it in
season two in the lobby of

00:01:52.297 --> 00:01:53.464
Jerry Daycare.

00:01:54.318 --> 00:01:56.992
(metal cranking)

00:01:56.992 --> 00:01:58.587
Not everything's purposeful, though.

00:01:58.587 --> 00:02:00.286
The theme song I actually
wrote for another show

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:01.803
that didn't get picked up,

00:02:01.803 --> 00:02:03.593
we used it as temporary
music for Rick and Morty,

00:02:03.593 --> 00:02:07.760
and everyone liked it so
we just decided to keep it.

00:02:09.726 --> 00:02:12.278
So I've been with Rick and
Morty since before the beginning,

00:02:12.278 --> 00:02:15.032
and I've seen all sorts
of music requests come in.

00:02:15.032 --> 00:02:17.658
And sometimes it gets a
little weird, but, you know,

00:02:17.658 --> 00:02:20.520
I like weird, and I wouldn't
have it any other way.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:23.236
- Ryan, I really wanna
do a rap about the flu,

00:02:23.236 --> 00:02:25.653
and can you do it this tempo?

00:02:26.512 --> 00:02:29.012
(beat boxing)

00:02:33.192 --> 00:02:37.359
Whatever that tempo is,
but give me five versions.

